  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of the kinds of structural and chemical phenomena that can occur in halide perovskites. A repeating lattice of corner-sharing metal–halide octahedra forms the backbone, with A-site cations shown in the cavities. Across this framework, the image highlights several ways the material can be modified or can evolve: substitutional doping and A-site cation mixing, molecular passivation layers on the surface, and redox changes within the lattice.\n\nIt also emphasizes interactions between the inorganic framework and attached organic molecules through halogen and hydrogen bonding, suggesting how surface treatments or spacers can stabilize or alter the crystal. In the lower middle, layered spacer molecules appear to create lower-dimensional structures, while red arrows indicate motion or reorientation of both spacer molecules and A-site cations, pointing to dynamic behavior rather than a rigid lattice.\n\nOn the right, the figure shows different halide coordination environments and halide dynamics, again using arrows to indicate local motion or exchange. The final region points to deviations from ideal cuboctahedral symmetry, implying distortions of the perovskite cage. Overall, the image summarizes the key compositional, interfacial, redox, and dynamic degrees of freedom in halide perovskite materials.",
